<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="plugin_editor_persistence">

  <!-- Proposal 33 D2 - a project saved with a plugin editor open opens with it
       open again.

       THE SPLIT IS THE DESIGN, and it is the same one proposal 37 P7 made for
       MIDI ports:

         WHETHER the editor was open  ->  the project (<SPluginSlot
                                          editorOpen='true'>), because "this
                                          project opens with the synth's editor
                                          up" means the same thing on any
                                          machine;
         WHERE the window was         ->  SSettings, per user, keyed by plugin
                                          uid, because monitor layout is
                                          machine-local and a window restored
                                          at x=2400 on a laptop is a window
                                          nobody can reach.

       This case gates the FIRST half. The second half is
       plugin_editor_geometry_test, which pins the off-screen clamp - the part
       that cannot be reproduced by hand without unplugging a monitor.

       What is being gated:

       1. The flag starts false, and OPENING the editor sets it. It is not an
          action and leaves no undo entry: opening a window is not an edit to
          the arrangement.

       2. It SURVIVES a save and a load. `editorOpen` is written only when
          true, which is what keeps every project file saved before D2 - and
          both render goldens - byte-unchanged; the `editorOpen=0` assertions
          below are therefore also asserting an ABSENT attribute.

       3. CLOSING the editor clears it, so a project saved after the user
          closed the window does not re-open it.

       4. THE RESTORE WALK IS A NO-OP IN A TEST-CASE RUN, and step 6 drives the
          real `SPluginNativeEditor::restoreOpenEditors()` to prove it. This is
          not tidiness: a qxa run uses the REAL platform plugin (the suite does
          not set QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen), so an unguarded restore would put
          plugin windows on the developer's desktop in the middle of a suite.

       5. A headless run writes NO editor geometry into the shared smaragd.ini.
          The window is opened with showWindow = false and so was never mapped;
          storing its geometry would overwrite the developer's real remembered
          position with a rectangle no user ever chose. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.gui"
                   name="Smaragd Test GUI" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>

    <!-- 1. Nothing is open, so nothing is flagged. -->
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" contains="editorOpen=0"/>

    <plugin-native-editor trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" action="open" expectOpen="1"/>
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" contains="editorOpen=1"/>

    <!-- 2. Save WHILE it is open, then take it down. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/plugin_editor_persistence.qxp"/>
    <plugin-native-editor trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" action="close" expectOpen="0"/>

    <!-- 3. ...and closing cleared the flag on the live model. -->
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" contains="editorOpen=0"/>

    <load-project path="../../build/plugin_editor_persistence.qxp"/>

    <!-- 2 (cont.) The saved project remembers it. -->
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0"
                         contains="name=Smaragd Test GUI|state=Active"/>
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" contains="editorOpen=1"/>

    <!-- 4. The real post-load walk, and it must open NOTHING here. Remove the
         isTestCaseMode() guard and this line fails with the editor open. -->
    <plugin-native-editor trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" action="restore" expectOpen="0"/>

    <!-- ...and the flag is still what the file said: a suppressed restore must
         not look like a user closing the window. -->
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" contains="editorOpen=1"/>

    <!-- 5. Nothing of OURS reached the user's INI: a headless open
         (showWindow = false) was never on screen, so it has no geometry worth
         keeping and saveGeometryToSettings() must decline to invent one.

         THE KEY IS SPELLED OUT, and the bare `editorGeometry` this used to
         match is what a scoped assertion exists to avoid. `smaragd.ini` is the
         REAL per-user file, shared by every build and every interactive
         session on the machine, and `editorGeometry\<format>%3A<uid>` is a key
         the app legitimately writes whenever a human opens a plugin's window
         and closes it. So the unscoped form failed on any developer box where
         that had ever happened - observed 2026-08-21 against a
         `vst3%3A...` entry from a real session, with nothing wrong in the code
         under test. Every other INI-touching case declares the keys it OWNS
         and restores them; this one owns exactly one, the CLAP fixture's, and
         that is the one it may speak about.

         The spelling is the ON-DISK one (`assert-settings-file` reads the file,
         not QSettings): `pluginui/editorGeometry/clap:tw.test.clap.gui` is
         written under `[pluginui]` as the key below, with QSettings' own
         percent-escape for the colon. -->
    <assert-settings-file absent="editorGeometry\clap%3Atw.test.clap.gui"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
